 ## Overview
-The Sphere Signed Distance Function (SDF) computes the exact Euclidean distance from a query point in 3D space to the surface of a sphere, returning a negative value if the point is inside and a positive value if outside [S1, S2, S3, S4, S5, S6, S7, S10, S15, S16, unofficial][S17][S18, S19, unofficial]. It serves as the fundamental building block for distance-field ray marching, allowing for efficient, adaptive step sizes that guarantee surface intersection without overshooting [S1, S10, S15, unofficial]. Unlike generic implicit functions (e.g., the quadratic form $f(p) = \|p-c\|^2 - r^2$) which require normalization to derive distances or normals, the SDF provides the metric directly [S1, S9, unofficial]. It is the 3D analog of the Circle SDF [G].
+The Sphere Signed Distance Function (SDF) computes the exact Euclidean distance from a query point in 3D space to the surface of a sphere, returning a negative value if the point is inside and a positive value if outside [S1, S2, S3, S4, S5, S6, S7, S10, S15, S16, unofficial][S17, unofficial][S18][S19, unofficial]. It serves as the fundamental building block for distance-field ray marching, enabling adaptive step sizes that guarantee surface intersection without overshooting when the step size equals the computed distance [S1, S10, S15, unofficial]. It is the 3D analog of the Circle SDF [G].
 
