 ## Background
-
-The Sphere SDF is one of the most basic and fundamental Signed Distance Functions, originating from the field of geometric modeling and ray marching. It was popularized in the context of ray marching by Inigo Quilez, who compiled a comprehensive list of distance functions for various primitives [S1, S10, S14, unofficial]. The mathematical basis for sphere tracing using SDFs was established by T. A. Nylander in "Sphere tracing: a geometric method for the antialiased ray tracing of implicit surfaces" (1999) [S17]. The function is widely used in real-time graphics, procedural generation, and implicit surface rendering due to its simplicity and efficiency [S1, S10, S15, unofficial]. It is also used in neural implicit representations (e.g., NeRFs) as a learnable primitive [S4, S5, S6, unofficial].
